{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: \nThe component implements the core mechanics of a WAI-ARIA live region (`role=\"status\"`, `aria-live=\"polite\"`, `aria-atomic=\"true\"`) and semantic list output, but it contains severe design flaws in state communication and semantic HTML. Most notably, the live region logic triggers premature \"No results found\" announcements on every keystroke while the user is actively typing, and the CSS uses `display: none` on `<label>` under the mistaken assumption that it remains accessible, stripping persistent visible labeling and deleting the native label from the accessibility tree.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n- **Live region timing and noise**: Live regions frequently suffer from race conditions or premature triggering before submission. *Confirmed: condition triggers on keystroke before submit.*\n- **Hidden label anti-pattern**: Developers frequently hide labels using `display: none` instead of visually-hidden utility classes. *Confirmed: CSS line 9 explicitly uses `display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */`.*\n- **Name calculation conflict**: Coexistence of `<label htmlFor=\"...\">` and `aria-label` creating naming divergence. *Confirmed: `<label>` text is \"Search\", while `aria-label` is \"Search query\".*\n- **Missing search landmark**: Forms used for search often omit `role=\"search\"` or native `<input type=\"search\">`. *Confirmed: `<form>` lacks role, input is `type=\"text\"`.*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n*No complete access blockers identified (the input remains operable via keyboard and has an `aria-label` fallback name).*\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n#### 1. Spurious and Premature Live Region Announcements During Input Entry\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:49` and `SearchResults.jsx:66-68`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users, Cognitive accessibility users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA), WCAG 3.2.2 On Input (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Status Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: In `SearchResults.jsx:49`, the condition `{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && 'No results found'}` evaluates to `true` the moment the user types the first character into the search box (before pressing Enter or clicking Search). The live region immediately announces *\"No results found\"* while the user is mid-keystroke. Simultaneously, lines 66\u201368 render the visual text `<p className=\"no-results\">No results found for \"{query}\"</p>`, disorienting cognitive users and misleading screen reader users into believing a query has executed and failed.\n- **Fix**: Introduce a dedicated submission/execution state (e.g., `hasSearched` or `submittedQuery`). Only announce and render \"No results found\" after an actual search request completes:\n  ```jsx\n  const [hasSearched, setHasSearched] = useState(false);\n\n  const handleSearch = async (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    if (!query.trim()) return;\n    setHasSearched(true);\n    setIsLoading(true);\n    // ...\n  };\n\n  // In live region:\n  {!isLoading && hasSearched && results.length === 0 && 'No results found'}\n  ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: live-region-premature-keystroke-announcement\nfingerprint: c4b18f0a2e7d8199\nsource: SearchResults.jsx:47-50\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages, WCAG 3.2.2 On Input\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, cognitive=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, vestibular=LOW, auditory=LOW, environmental_contrast=LOW\nevidence: SearchResults.jsx:49: `{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && 'No results found'}` evaluated during `onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}`\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Focus #search-input with screen reader enabled (NVDA/VoiceOver).\n  2. Type a single character \"a\". Do not press Enter or click Search.\n  3. Observe live region output.\nexpected_behavior: Live region remains silent while user types, announcing results count or status only after form submission.\nactual_behavior: Live region immediately broadcasts \"No results found\" on the first character entered.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. CSS `display: none` on `<label>` Destroys Label Semantics and Strips Visible Label\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.css:8-10` and `SearchResults.jsx:28`\n- **Impacted Users**: Low vision users, Cognitive accessibility users, Speech recognition users, Screen reader users\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), WCAG 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions (Level A), WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name (Level A)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: CSS rule `.search-container label { display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */ }` embodies a dangerous misconception: `display: none` completely removes elements from the browser accessibility tree across all modern user agents. Screen readers ignore `display: none` nodes entirely. Furthermore, relying purely on `placeholder=\"Search for...\"` deprives sighted, low-vision, and cognitive users of a persistent visible label once text is entered.\n- **Fix**: Remove `display: none`. Retain a visible `<label>`, or if a visually-hidden label is an intentional visual design requirement, apply the existing `.sr-only` class to the `<label>` instead of `display: none`:\n  ```jsx\n  <label htmlFor=\"search-input\" className=\"sr-only\">Search</label>\n  ```\n  *(Best practice: keep the label visible above or beside the input for cognitive and low-vision clarity).*\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: display-none-on-html-label\nfingerprint: e7302bd91a44f301\nsource: SearchResults.css:8-10\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, WCAG 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, vestibular=LOW, auditory=LOW, environmental_contrast=LOW\nevidence: SearchResults.css:8-10: `.search-container label { display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */ }`\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Inspect accessibility tree of `#search-input` in Chrome DevTools / Safari Accessibility Inspector.\n  2. Verify if `<label>` contributes to accessible name computation.\nexpected_behavior: Accessible name computed via associated `<label>` element.\nactual_behavior: `<label>` is omitted from accessibility tree due to `display: none`.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n1. **Accessible Name Conflict (`aria-label` overriding `<label>`)**\n   - **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:28, 35`\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name, WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\n   - **Details**: The `<input>` defines both an associated `<label>` (\"Search\") and `aria-label=\"Search query\"`. Under the Accessible Name and Description Computation (AccName) specification, `aria-label` takes precedence and suppresses the native label text. If the label were visible, speech-to-text users commanding \"Click Search\" would encounter an activation mismatch.\n   - **Fix**: Remove `aria-label=\"Search query\"` from `<input>` and let `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>` supply the accessible name natively.\n\n2. **Missing Search Landmark Role and Input Semantics**\n   - **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:27, 31`\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, WAI-ARIA Landmarks\n   - **Details**: The `<form>` lacks `role=\"search\"`. Screen reader users navigating by landmark regions cannot quickly jump to the search mechanism. In addition, `type=\"text\"` should be `type=\"search\"` to allow virtual keyboards to display the appropriate action button and provide native search semantics.\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"search\"` to `<form>` and change `type=\"text\"` to `type=\"search\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n- **Missing `aria-busy` State During Network Latency**: While `isLoading` is true, the results container or form does not communicate `aria-busy=\"true\"`. Adding `aria-busy={isLoading}` to the container prevents assistive technologies from attempting to read mutating DOM structures prematurely.\n- **Search Results Actionability**: The items in `<ul className=\"results-list\">` render static `<h3>` and `<p>` text with no interactive links (`<a>`). If these are intended to link to target pages, ensure semantic anchor elements are used inside the list items.\n- **Search Result Heading Association**: The `<h2>Search Results</h2>` heading should be programmatically associated with the list container via `aria-labelledby` or wrapped in a section with an accessible name.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (gaps and unstated assumptions)\n- **Clear / Reset Mechanism**: No button to clear the search input and reset query state easily for keyboard or screen reader users.\n- **Error Handling Region**: If the simulated API call fails (network timeout or error), there is no error state communicated visually or via `aria-live=\"assertive\"`.\n- **Focus Indicator Definition**: The CSS does not explicitly define `:focus-visible` styles with a minimum 3:1 contrast ratio against the background (WCAG 2.4.7 / 2.4.13), relying on browser defaults.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen Reader User**:\n  - The live region configuration (`role=\"status\"`, `aria-live=\"polite\"`, `aria-atomic=\"true\"`) is mechanically valid, but the execution logic sends premature failure announcements while typing.\n  - The `<label>` is invisible to the accessibility tree due to `display: none`.\n- **Keyboard-Only User**:\n  - Form submission via Enter key and Tab navigation through the form button works naturally via native HTML elements.\n  - Focus remains on the submit button after search; since results appear directly following the form in DOM order, Tab moves smoothly into the results list.\n- **Low Vision User (200% zoom, high contrast)**:\n  - Complete absence of a persistent visible label relies on placeholder text, which vanishes upon typing.\n  - Color contrast for `#0066cc` on `#ffffff` is ~5.6:1 (passes WCAG AA).\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**:\n  - Typing in an empty query immediately flashes \"No results found for '[char]'\" underneath the form before pressing search, creating confusion about system state.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | `display: none` label stripping; premature live region broadcast on keystroke |\n| **Cognitive** | **HIGH** | \"No results found\" renders immediately upon typing first character |\n| **Low vision** | **MEDIUM** | Missing persistent visible input label (placeholder reliance) |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **LOW** | Native `<form>`, `<input>`, and `<button>` handle keyboard activation correctly |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animated transitions, carousels, or motion triggers present |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No media or audio content involved |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **LOW** | Brand blue `#0066cc` meets 4.5:1 text threshold against white |\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n**Verdict: REVISE.**\nWhile the component utilizes native form controls and sets up a proper polite live region container, two design bugs undermine its accessibility:\n1. Broadcasting \"No results found\" during raw keystroke changes degrades the screen reader and cognitive experience.\n2. Relying on `display: none` for `<label>` deletes native label semantics from the accessibility tree and deprives users of a visible label.\n\nAddressing these issues requires state-logic decoupling (`hasSearched` state flag) and CSS/markup alignment (using `.sr-only` or visible label styling instead of `display: none`), at which point the component will fully meet WCAG 2.2 AA and WAI-ARIA APG standards.\n",
